  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a latch and a flip-flop?",
      "a": "A latch follows its inputs whenever it is enabled, for as long as the enable level is held. A flip-flop changes only at a clock edge, the instant the clock rises or falls, which keeps large circuits in step.",
      "asks": [
        "Latch vs flip-flop?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does an SR latch remember?",
      "a": "Two cross-coupled gates feed each other's outputs back as inputs. Once set, the loop keeps Q at 1 even after S returns to 0, until a reset pulse flips it.",
      "asks": [
        "What is feedback in a latch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why is S = R = 1 not allowed in an SR latch?",
      "a": "With NOR gates it forces both Q and not Q to 0, so they are no longer opposites, and if both inputs return to 0 together the final state depends on which gate is faster. Designs avoid it, for example by using a D flip-flop.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the forbidden state of an SR latch?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does a D flip-flop work?",
      "a": "At each rising clock edge it copies the D input to Q, then holds that value until the next edge, ignoring any changes to D in between.",
      "asks": [
        "What does a D flip-flop do on the clock edge?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is setup time?",
      "a": "Setup time is how long the D input must be steady before the clock edge for the flip-flop to capture it reliably. Hold time is how long it must stay steady after the edge. Breaking them can leave the output undecided, called metastable.",
      "asks": [
        "What are setup and hold times?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A standard SRAM memory cell uses six transistors: two cross-coupled inverters and two access transistors.",
    "William Eccles and F. W. Jordan invented the flip-flop circuit in 1918, using vacuum tubes.",
    "An edge-triggered D flip-flop updates its output only at a clock edge, usually the rising edge."
  ],
